#Title running lights
#Composer SuperForge (procedural)
#Tempo 78

; A DRIVING piece, for the action rails. `slice_b_song` stays what it is --
; the room rail's A/B reverb demonstration is calibrated to that song's
; 768-tick loop and its composed rest half-bar (tests/test_slice_b_audio.py),
; and a piece with a drum kit under it has no such window. Two rails, two
; jobs, two songs.
;
; A tighter echo than room A's: 64 ms of buffer (8 KiB of ARAM instead of 16)
; behind a low-passed FIR, so the tail is a slap that darkens rather than a
; cavern that smears the hats. Taps sum to 127 -- the compiler rejects an
; absolute sum over 128.
;
; EVOL AND EFB ARE NOT FREE, and this song was authored at 10/20 until the
; landing gate said otherwise. 12/24 is a TREE-WIDE constant: it is the dry
; rest state that two sound effects WRITE BACK literally -- `room_a_ambience`
; when the player leaves the cavern, and `beam_end` when the saucer's beam
; stops swelling the arena (both in sound-effects.txt). An effect that
; restores a value the running song never had does not restore anything; it
; moves the rail to a rest state it was never in, permanently, after the
; first use. boss_saucer's echo cycle caught this: its arena rested at
; 10/20, the beam swelled it to 70/96, and `beam_end` would have settled it
; to 12/24 and left it there.
;
; The buffer length and the FIR are this song's own, because nothing writes
; them back: the vendored compiler structurally refuses `set_echo_delay`
; inside a sound effect (sound-effects.txt's header says so), and no effect
; touches the FIR taps. So the character survives; only the two numbers under
; contract are given up.
#EchoLength 64
#EchoVolume 12
#EchoFeedback 24
#FirFilter 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 0

; D minor. The key signature flats every `b` so the mode is written, not
; spelled out note by note; `c+` in the A-chord bars is the raised leading
; tone that pulls the turnaround back to D.
#KeySignature d

@1 saw
@2 square_lead
@3 tri_bass
@4 step
@5 kick

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DRUMS DO NOT TOUCH THE NOISE GENERATOR. There is exactly one, and the
; driver zeroes the volume of every music channel playing noise the moment a
; sound effect wants it (audio-driver.asm, `nonShadow_music` under the
; `SfxNoise` branch). A noise hi-hat would therefore drop out under every
; explosion -- which on the shmup is most of the time. So the kit is samples:
; `kick` is a pitch-dropping one-shot written for this, and `step` (the
; footstep burst) is the snare at o3 and the hat at o5.
;
; K0 disables key-off on both drum channels: these are one-shots that decay
; to zero on their own, and a key-off would choke the kick's tail. Gaps are
; therefore `w` waits, never `r` rests -- a rest still keys off under K0.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!h  v7  o5 c%12
!s  v14 o3 g%12
!e  !h !h !s !h !h !h !s !h
!k1 o3 c%36 c%12 c%48
!k2 o3 c%48 c%24 c%12 c%12

; The bass and the mid pulse are the same 16 bars of harmony, so they are
; written once each as a one-bar figure and moved by driver transpose. The
; figure is a root-fifth pair for the pulse and a root gallop for the bass;
; transposition preserves the interval, so every chord comes out right
; without a second copy of the rhythm.
;   Dm _0 | Bb _-4 | C _-2 | Gm _+5 | A _+7
!bb o2 [d%6 d%6 >d%6 <d%6]4
!pp o3 [d%12 a%12]4

; ---- A: lead. Saw, wet, vibrato. Rests the first two bars so the groove
;         states itself before the tune arrives.
A  L @1 v11 E1 Q7 MP20,l16,l16 l8
A  r1                              | r2 r4 o4 a >c
A  o5 d4. c8 d4 f4                 | e2 d8 c8 o4 a b
A  o5 c4. d8 e4 c4                 | d2. c8 o4 b
A  o5 d4 c4 o4 a4 f4               | e2 c+4 e4
A  o4 g4. a8 >c4 <b4               | o5 d2 c8 <b8 a8 g8
A  o4 f4. g8 a4 >d4                | o5 c2. <b8 a8
A  o5 e4. d8 c4 <g4                | o5 c2 d8 e8 g8 e8
A  o5 f4 e4 d4 <a4                 | o4 c+2 e4 a4

; ---- B: mid pulse. Root and fifth in eighths, dry, panned left. Holds off
;         for two bars so the top of every loop is bass and kit alone.
B  L @2 v7 E0 Q5 p40
B  r1 r1 _-4 !pp !pp _-2 !pp !pp _0 !pp _+7 !pp
B  _+5 !pp !pp _-4 !pp !pp _-2 !pp !pp _0 !pp _+7 !pp

; ---- C: bass. Sixteenth gallop with the octave on every third, dry.
C  L @3 v12 E0 Q6
C  _0 !bb !bb _-4 !bb !bb _-2 !bb !bb _0 !bb _+7 !bb
C  _+5 !bb !bb _-4 !bb !bb _-2 !bb !bb _0 !bb _+7 !bb

; ---- D: arpeggio. A broken chord per bar at a 32nd, quiet, panned right,
;         held by an ADSR that does not decay so the bar rings evenly. It
;         fills the RIFF half and leaves the melody half alone -- with every
;         part sounding for all sixteen bars the loop has no shape, and the
;         two halves are otherwise distinguished only by their chords.
D  L @2 v5 E1 A15,7,6,0 p96 o4
D  {{dfa}}1,%3 {{dfa}}1,%3 {{dfb}}1,%3 {{dfb}}1,%3
D  {{ceg}}1,%3 {{ceg}}1,%3 {{dfa}}1,%3 {{c+ea}}1,%3
D  r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1 r1

; ---- E: snare and hat.   ---- F: kick.
; Nothing is written to G or H: those are the two channels the driver ducks
; while a sound effect plays, so leaving them empty means no part of the
; song disappears when the game makes a noise.
E  L @4 K0 E0
E  [!e ]16
F  L @5 v16 K0 E0
F  [!k1 !k2 ]8
